---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const files = `${base}examples/skysynth/`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Example: SkySynth"
  description="SkyDiscover's SkySynth multi-agent synthesis workflow, written as an OMAR program on Claude and Codex."
>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    <a
      href="https://github.com/skydiscover-ai/skydiscover"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">SkySynth</a
    >
    builds a system for your workload with a team of agents: a spec builder, a planner,
    a coder, evaluators, an auditor and a critic, each started fresh for its step.
    This page runs it as one OMAR program, so the order of steps, the gates between
    them and which agent may speak when are fixed by the program instead of by a lead
    agent's judgment.
  </p>

  <video
    controls
    muted
    playsinline
    preload="metadata"
    src={`${files}skysynth.mp4`}></video>
  <p class="caption">
    A real run on the tutorial cache task: live Claude and Codex agents, 32
    minutes, shown sped up.
  </p>

  <h2>What it shows</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>SkySynth needs</th><th>OMAR construct</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>A fresh agent for every step, but one lead throughout</td><td
          ><code>coder : Codex per task</code>; the lead has no <code>per</code
          ></td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>A model per role (Opus lead, Sonnet loop)</td><td
          ><code>Claude(model = "claude-sonnet-5-5")</code></td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td
          >Delivery refused until every kept test passes, fixes in the same
          conversation</td
        ><td
          ><code>check "bash delivery_check_wrap.sh tutorial"</code> on the coder's
          task</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>CLI hooks (clone-reuse guard) loaded before the agent starts</td
        ><td
          ><code>setup "bash install.sh --agent codex ."</code> on the team</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Score only after the test gate</td><td
          >Reaction order, proved with <code>omarc --prove</code></td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The coder's team, as it appears in the generated program (the long shared
    context string is abbreviated as <code>$(ctx)</code>):
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`team Coder(ctx : string)[coder : Codex per task]
{
    setup "bash .../install.sh --agent codex ."
    input iter_job : string
    output changed : string
    task coder(iter_job) -> changed within(3h)
    "$(ctx) Role: coding-agent, iteration mode: one change, fast tests, exit. ..."
    check "bash .../delivery_check_wrap.sh tutorial"
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Backend, model</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr></thead
    >
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>lead</td><td>Claude, claude-opus-5-5</td><td>whole run</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>spec builder (discovery, environment, requirements, hardening)</td
        ><td>Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>kb builder (child team, parallel with requirements)</td><td
          >Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td
        ><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>planner</td><td>Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>coder (bootstrap, then one change per iteration)</td><td
          >Codex, default model</td
        ><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>evaluator (correctness, performance)</td><td
          >Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td
        ><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>auditor (on every new best)</td><td>Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td
        ><td>per task</td></tr
      >
      <tr><td>critic</td><td>Claude, claude-sonnet-5-5</td><td>per task</td></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    Tutorial cache task (<code>workload.py</code>), 3 iterations, 32 minutes.
    Each iteration beat the last; the best cache scores 0.9327 against 0.3886
    for FIFO, and an independent replay of the exported artifact gives the same
    number.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead
      ><tr
        ><th>Iteration</th><th>app_hit_rate (seed 7)</th><th
          >Held-out (seed 123)</th
        ><th>FIFO (seed 7)</th></tr
      ></thead
    >
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>0.9195</td><td>0.9212</td><td>0.3886</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>0.9215</td><td>0.9232</td><td>0.3886</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 (best)</td><td>0.9327</td><td>0.9348</td><td>0.3886</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li>
      20 role tasks ran in 20 fresh conversations, all stopped afterwards.
    </li>
    <li>
      The delivery check ran 4 times (3 coder iterations and the final report)
      and passed each time. 5 kept tests are each shown to catch a mutant.
    </li>
    <li>The auditor ran on each of the 3 new bests, before the critic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What can go wrong</h2>
  <p>
    A SkySynth run is hours of agents working unattended, held together by one
    lead agent that follows <code>SKILL.md</code>. Two failures of that design
    cost the most, and the OMAR program rules both out.
  </p>

  <h3>A candidate scored before its tests pass</h3>
  <p>
    Each iteration must run the kept tests on the coder's change before the
    performance evaluator scores it. In SkySynth that order holds as long as the
    lead follows it; a delivery hook also runs the tests, but only on CLIs that
    load hooks. A broken but fast candidate that gets scored can become the
    best, and the next iterations build on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the program, the evaluator's only input, <code>gated</code>, is written
    by the reaction that runs the tests and stops the run if any fails.
    <code>omarc --prove</code> proves that every run of the program respects this:
    the evaluator is never invoked unless the gate completed and wrote
    <code>gated</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`$ omarc --prove SkySynth.omar scoring-proof.json verdicts.json
proved 1 of 1 properties of SkySynth.omar`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The property, as a Lean theorem about every run (reaction 22 is the test
    gate, reaction 23 the performance evaluator):
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`theorem scoring_follows_the_test_gate (e : Execution) (hv : Valid topology e) :
  ∀ g, e.invoked g "synth.reaction.23" →
    ∃ g', e.completed g' "synth.reaction.22" ∧
          (e.wrote g' "synth.reaction.22" "synth.gated").isSome`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Let the evaluator also start on another port that skips the gate, and the
    same proof is rejected. Proof file:
    <a href={`${files}scoring-proof.json`}>scoring-proof.json</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>The lead's context fills up</h3>
  <p>
    In SkySynth every role reports back to the lead, which runs the whole loop
    in one conversation, so its context grows with every iteration. SkySynth's
    <a
      href="https://github.com/skydiscover-ai/skydiscover/blob/main/skydiscover/synthesize/workflow/scripts/unattended/supervisor.sh"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">supervisor script</a
    >
    watches for "100% context used", compacts the lead, and starts a fresh lead when
    compacting stops helping. Either way the lead then knows the run only from a summary
    or the files on disk, including which iteration it is on and whether an audit
    is owed.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the program the lead has 3 tasks in the whole run: report the knowledge
    base's status, settle the property questions, and write the final report.
    That stays 3 whatever the iteration budget. The loop is the program's: the
    iteration count is program state (<code>k</code> of <code>n</code>), the
    audit decision is a script reading the leaderboard, and each role runs in a
    fresh conversation (20 role tasks in 20 conversations in the run above).
    Nothing about where the run is lives in an agent's context.
  </p>

  <h3>Also handled</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>What can go wrong</th><th>What OMAR does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>An agent's process dies mid-task</td><td
          >Starts a new agent on the same task, up to 2 retries within the
          task's deadline. The lead resumes its own conversation; a
          <code>per task</code> role starts fresh.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td
          >An agent stops without finishing: says it's done without completing,
          or asks a question nobody will answer</td
        ><td
          >Sends one reminder naming the open task and its outputs (a question
          is answered with it). If the agent stops again, it is restarted as
          above.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>An agent hits its plan's usage limit</td><td
          >Reads the limit and its reset time from the CLI, stops the task's
          deadline clock, and neither reminds nor restarts the agent. After the
          reset the same task continues.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>A role is lost when the lead's turn ends</td><td
          >There is no lead turn to end: each role is an agent the runtime
          supervises.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>The lead skips a role or does its job</td><td
          >The runtime starts each role when its inputs arrive; the lead has no
          say.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The first three, shown with real Codex agents on small programs (not
    SkySynth):
  </p>
  <video
    controls
    muted
    playsinline
    preload="metadata"
    poster={`${files}worker-restart.jpg`}
    src={`${files}worker-restart.mp4`}></video>
  <p class="caption">
    A coder's Codex process is killed mid-task. OMAR starts a new one that
    resumes the same conversation and finishes. The resumed work is sped up 4×.
  </p>
  <video
    controls
    muted
    playsinline
    preload="metadata"
    poster={`${files}worker-remind.jpg`}
    src={`${files}worker-remind.mp4`}></video>
  <p class="caption">
    A writer asks the user a question and waits. OMAR answers with a reminder;
    the writer picks an answer itself and completes. Not sped up.
  </p>
  <video
    controls
    muted
    playsinline
    preload="metadata"
    poster={`${files}usage-limit.jpg`}
    src={`${files}usage-limit.mp4`}></video>
  <p class="caption">
    An agent with <code>within(15s)</code> hits a usage limit that resets in 35 s.
    The model provider is simulated locally, so no real plan is used. OMAR waits past
    the deadline without a reminder, restart or failure, and the same task completes.
    Not sped up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Restarts, reminders and usage-limit waits are in
    <a
      href="https://github.com/omar-os/omar/pull/414"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">#414</a
    >,
    <a
      href="https://github.com/omar-os/omar/pull/415"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">#415</a
    >
    and
    <a
      href="https://github.com/omar-os/omar/pull/416"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">#416</a
    >, not yet in a release.
  </p>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <p>
    Needs OMAR with <code>per</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>check</code> and
    <code>setup</code> (not yet in a release), Claude Code and Codex signed in, Python
    3 and jq, and a checkout of SkyDiscover. The program is generated for your paths:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`mkdir skysynth && cd skysynth
for f in gen.py helper.sh helper_dry.sh delivery_check_wrap.sh; do
  curl -fsSLO https://omar.rs/examples/skysynth/$f
done
python3 gen.py ~/skydiscover
omar up -s skysynth
omar -s skysynth run SkySynth.omar \\
  --input synth.ask="Build me a fast cache for the workload in workload.py." \\
  --input synth.budget=3 --timeout-seconds 86400 --checkpoint-period tag --keep-workspaces`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>python3 gen.py ~/skydiscover --stub</code> writes
    <code>SkySynthStub.omar</code>, which runs the same control flow on the Stub
    backend in under a minute with no model calls. Agents work on a read-only
    copy of SkyDiscover; a check after every role stops the run if the copy or
    your checkout changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Files: <a href={`${files}gen.py`}>gen.py</a>,
    <a href={`${files}helper.sh`}>helper.sh</a>,
    <a href={`${files}delivery_check_wrap.sh`}>delivery_check_wrap.sh</a>,
    <a href={`${files}helper_dry.sh`}>helper_dry.sh</a>,
    <a href={`${files}scoring-proof.json`}>scoring-proof.json</a>, and the
    program as generated for <code>/home/you</code>:
    <a href={`${files}SkySynth.omar`}>SkySynth.omar</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Differences from SkySynth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Smoke budget: 3 iterations (SkySynth's Quick is about 20). The production
      lenses (security, reliability, attack) are not run, so the result is a
      scoped run, not production-ready.
    </li>
    <li>
      No web panels: the ask and budget are run inputs, and the lead answers
      property questions with their declared defaults.
    </li>
    <li>
      The kb builder runs alongside the requirements step and finishes before
      the loop, instead of in the background.
    </li>
    <li>
      OMAR cannot set per-agent environment variables, so each prompt exports
      the <code>SKYDISCOVER_*</code> variables itself.
    </li>
    <li>Claude and Codex only.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>

